+### 2352 — The agy preflight has no home in the plan surface, so the evidence travels on the plugin
+- DECISION: `agy.New()` carries no preflight evidence and `agy.NewWithRuntime(Runtime{Executable})` carries it. That makes agy the ONE ported plugin holding state, and the divergence is stated on the type rather than discovered.
+- ROOT CAUSE: The source reads `cfg.AgyRuntime` — evidence hanging off its Config. This layer's `LaunchRequest` has no field for it and must not grow one: an agy-shaped field on the type every system reads would put one harness's preflight into the core contract for five plugins to ignore. `ProbeAgyRuntime` itself runs `agy --version` and `agy --help`, so it cannot live behind any `System` method: `BuildPlan` calls all of them to build a DRY RUN. Same boundary as claude's goal preparation, different mechanism.
+- FINDING: The source has TWO resolution functions with DIFFERENT refusal behaviour — `resolveAgyBinary` hard-fails with no evidence, `resolveAgyBinaryForDisplay` falls back to the bare `agy` placeholder — and this layer's `ResolveBinary` takes NO MODE, deliberately, because that is what guarantees a dry run cannot drift from the launch target. The two map onto one mode-less resolution plus one mode-aware refusal in `Argv`, which is the only plugin surface that knows the mode. Dropping the refusal instead would have been a validating gate getting weaker across a move: agy has no PATH fallback at all, so an exec plan carrying the placeholder execs whatever PATH holds.
+- NOTE: `init()` registers the PREFLIGHTLESS value, and that is behavioural rather than incidental. init has no context, no deadline and nowhere to report a probe failure, so a registration that probed would make importing the package start processes. `TestTheDefaultRegistryHoldsThePreflightlessValue` drives an exec plan through `agentic.Default` and requires the refusal.
+- NOTE: The dry run's no-side-effect promise is MEASURED, not asserted: a plan builds over an assignment file that does not exist and an environment carrying no PATH, and the same request under exec must fail — otherwise the dry run's success proves nothing about doing less work.
+- SCOPE: `pkg/agentic/systems/agy/{runtime.go,binary.go,agy.go,args.go}`, `agy_test.go`, `parity_test.go`.
+- STATUS: TASK-260822-xz8rj5 handed to review, uncommitted for the CR snapshot.
+
+### 2350 — agy's effort is a MODEL-ID SUFFIX, and the plugin must never learn to read it
+- FINDING: The source registers agy models as `gemini-3.6-flash-high`, `gemini-3.1-pro-low`, `gemini-3.5-flash-medium`; every row is `ReasoningNone` and `buildAgyArgs` references no effort value. So "agy supports high effort" is a VENDOR-layer fact about a model row, not a transport this system has.
+- DECISION: The plugin declares `EffortTransportNone` and passes the model id through verbatim. That makes the refusal of a required-effort model decidable from the contract alone (`CanCarry` is false), and an explicit effort value is refused rather than dropped — which is the whole of "the effort flag is rejected" at this layer. Neither agy golden configures an effort, so both refusals have their own tests and a made-up suffix (`gemini-9.9-future-glacial`) pins the pass-through.
+- NOTE: A plugin that parsed the suffix would have to decide what to do with a word it does not know, and every answer to that is Layer 1 holding a Layer-2 opinion. The parity mutant that strips `-high` from the id is byte-identical in flag COUNT and fails only because the golden compares argument values.
+- SCOPE: `pkg/agentic/systems/agy/agy.go`, `args.go`, `agy_test.go`, `parity_test.go`.
+- STATUS: TASK-260822-xz8rj5 handed to review, uncommitted for the CR snapshot.
